<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Philippolis Griqua State</h1><p class="st-meta">1826 CE – 1861 CE (approx.) · capital Philippolis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Adam Kok's Griqua captaincy north of the Orange River, squeezed out by Boer settlers until its people trekked to Griqualand East.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippolis">Philippolis</a> began in 1823 as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london-missionary-society">London Missionary Society</a> station for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-people">San</a>. In 1826 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-kok-ii">Adam Kok II</a> and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griqua">Griqua</a> followers, who had split from the community at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griquatown">Griquatown</a>, settled there and made it the centre of their own captaincy.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Kok family ruled with a council and a mission-backed legal order, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griqua">Griqua</a> farmed sheep and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>. From the 1830s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> trekboers leased and bought Griqua farms. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-kok-iii">Adam Kok III</a>, kaptein from 1837, sought British support, signing a treaty with the Cape in 1843, but under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/orange-river-sovereignty">Orange River Sovereignty</a> and then the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/orange-free-state">Orange Free State</a> the Griqua steadily lost land.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1861 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-kok-iii">Adam Kok III</a> sold the remaining land to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/orange-free-state">Orange Free State</a> and led about 2,000 people across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drakensberg">Drakensberg</a> to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/griqualand-east">Nomansland</a>, where they founded Griqualand East.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1826 CE – 1861 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:96.04%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1826 CE – 1861 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Philippolis</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Adam Kok II, Adam Kok III</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Cape Dutch, Khoekhoe</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Adam Kok's Griqualand, Philippolis</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1844&amp;focus=philippolis-griqua-state" aria-label="Philippolis Griqua State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5336 3282 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Philippolis Griqua State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5656 3538L5684 3498M5656 3538L5763 3548"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5684 3498h0M5763 3548h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5656 3538h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5656 3538h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Philippolis. <a href="/atlas?year=1844&amp;focus=philippolis-griqua-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
